        ["Viruses are described as obligate intracellular parasites because they:", ["Need host cell machinery to replicate", "Live only inside the bloodstream", "Produce their own ATP inside host cells", "Divide by binary fission in the cytoplasm"], "Viruses lack ribosomes and metabolic enzymes, so they must hijack host cells to copy themselves. Binary fission describes bacteria."],
        ["Lipopolysaccharide released from gram-negative bacteria in the blood is MOST likely to cause:", ["Fever and hypotension that can progress to septic shock", "Localized muscle spasm at the site of bacterial entry", "Rapid sporulation of the bacteria within the bloodstream", "Immediate resistance of the bacteria to all antibiotics"], "Endotoxin triggers a massive inflammatory cytokine release, leading to fever, vasodilation, and hypotension. It does not cause sporulation or resistance."],
        ["Many antifungal drugs target ergosterol. Why does this work?", ["Ergosterol is in fungal membranes, while human cells use cholesterol", "Ergosterol forms the peptidoglycan wall that surrounds fungal cells", "Ergosterol is the fungal equivalent of a viral protein capsid", "Human cells use ergosterol only in the liver"], "Fungi use ergosterol in their membranes in place of cholesterol, giving a selective target. Because fungi are eukaryotes, selective targets are fewer than for bacteria."]
