      lesson: [
        "<b>Ecology</b> studies how organisms interact with each other and their environment, and human health sits inside that web. Pathogens have life cycles that involve animals, water, and soil. <b>Zoonoses</b> are infections that pass from animals to people, such as Lyme disease (tick-borne), rabies, and many emerging viruses. Climate and land use change shift where vectors such as ticks and mosquitoes live, which changes who gets sick and where. On the Gulf Coast, heat, humidity, flooding, and mold after storms are environmental health issues, not abstract ones.",
        "<b>Environmental health</b> examines how physical, chemical, and biological exposures affect people. Air pollution (fine particulate matter, ozone) worsens asthma and COPD and increases cardiovascular events. Lead exposure in older housing harms child neurodevelopment. Heat waves raise mortality, especially in older adults. Occupational exposures such as noise, vibration, repetitive lifting, and solvents produce hearing loss, musculoskeletal disorders, and neuropathies. The <b>social determinants of health</b> (where people live, work, learn, and play, along with income, education, and access to care) shape exposure and outcomes as much as biology does.",
        "<b>Epidemiology</b> is the study of the distribution and determinants of health and disease in populations. Two basic measures describe how common a condition is. <b>Prevalence</b> is the proportion of a population that has the condition at a given time: <span class='formula'>prevalence = existing cases / total population</span>. It is a snapshot. <b>Incidence</b> (cumulative incidence or risk) is the proportion of an at-risk population that develops new cases over a defined period: <span class='formula'>incidence = new cases / population at risk during the period</span>. It describes the flow of new cases.",
        "The two are linked. For a stable condition, <span class='formula'>prevalence &asymp; incidence x average duration</span>. A disease that is common but short (the cold) has high incidence and modest prevalence. A chronic disease (osteoarthritis, type 2 diabetes) accumulates cases, so prevalence can be high even when incidence is moderate. If a treatment keeps people alive longer without curing them, prevalence rises even though incidence did not change. If a disease becomes rapidly fatal or rapidly curable, prevalence falls.",
        "Worked example: a town has 20,000 adults. On January 1, 1,000 have chronic low back pain. Over the year, 950 of the 19,000 who did not have it develop it. Point prevalence on January 1 is 1,000 / 20,000 = 0.05, or 5%. One-year cumulative incidence is 950 / 19,000 = 0.05, or 5%. Note the denominator for incidence is the population <i>at risk</i>, which excludes people who already had the condition. Mixing these denominators is a common exam error.",
        "Other basic terms appear in every research article. <b>Morbidity</b> refers to illness and <b>mortality</b> to death; a mortality rate is deaths divided by the population over a period. An <b>epidemic</b> is an increase in cases above what is expected in a population; a <b>pandemic</b> is an epidemic that spreads across countries or continents; <b>endemic</b> means a constant presence at a baseline level. <b>Risk factors</b> are characteristics associated with a higher probability of disease, and prevention is described in levels: <b>primary</b> prevents disease before it occurs (vaccination, fall-prevention exercise for healthy older adults), <b>secondary</b> detects disease early to limit progression (screening), and <b>tertiary</b> reduces disability from established disease (cardiac rehab, rehab after stroke).",
        "For PTs, epidemiology justifies programs and guides screening. Knowing that falls are common and costly among older adults supports community balance classes, which are primary or secondary prevention depending on who attends. Knowing that a condition has high prevalence in your clinic population tells you which screening questions matter most. Reading incidence and prevalence correctly is the first step in reading the research that will guide your clinical decisions."
      ],
